# Line endings are LF everywhere, working tree included.
#
# This repository is built on Windows and ships to Linux, and the two disagreeing about line
# endings caused two real faults: a `.desktop` file with CRLF fails validation, which is what
# stops the game appearing in the application menu and therefore in Steam, and a shell script
# with CRLF fails on its own first line. It also made the build stamp useless — Windows git
# checks out CRLF while Linux git expects LF, so the same clean checkout reads as "dirty" from
# whichever side did not create it, and every build claimed to contain uncommitted work.
#
# Committing to LF in the working tree costs nothing: Rust, Cargo, MSVC and every editor worth
# using read LF on Windows without noticing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Committed binaries. Git detects these by itself, but a misdetected TTF is a font that will
# not parse and a misdetected WAV is a burst of noise, and neither failure points at line
# endings when you go looking for it.
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.ttc binary
*.wav binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary

# Files that end up on a Linux machine, and break there if they carry Windows line endings.
# A `.desktop` with CRLF fails validation, which is what stops the game appearing in the
# application menu — and therefore in Steam. A shell script with CRLF fails on its first line
# with `$'\r': command not found`. Both were shipped that way once.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.desktop   text eol=lf
*.metainfo.xml text eol=lf
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
